WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.602 --> 00:00:05.441
Hi everyone, well,

00:00:05.442 --> 00:00:07.547
it's my real pleasure to
introduce Sabrina Rashid.

00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:10.522
She is a PhD candidate at
Carnegie Mellon University

00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:12.152
working with Sid Fargosid.

00:00:12.153 --> 00:00:16.486
And she spent the summer looking
at applying deep learning to

00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:20.652
single cell data, in particular
using, as she'll say,

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:23.883
using new variational
autoencoders to try to

00:00:23.884 --> 00:00:28.491
understand the heterogeneity of
tumors from single cell data.

00:00:28.492 --> 00:00:32.922
And I look forward to
hearing what she has to say.

00:00:32.923 --> 00:00:34.311
Thank you.

00:00:35.518 --> 00:00:37.420
[LAUGH] Hi, everyone.

00:00:37.421 --> 00:00:39.806
As Rohu mentioned, I'm Sabrina,

00:00:39.807 --> 00:00:43.940
a finishing cardio PhD student
at Carnegie Mellon University

00:00:43.941 --> 00:00:47.175
Computational Biology
Department.

00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:49.683
So in this summer
intern project,

00:00:49.684 --> 00:00:52.540
we looked into single
cell tumor data.

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:56.252
And we know tumor comes from
very heterogeneous landscape, so

00:00:56.253 --> 00:00:58.983
we wanted to find what are the,
can we identify

00:00:58.984 --> 00:01:02.500
the underlying heterogeneity
from that single cell data.

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:05.465
So this is a brief
outline of the talk,

00:01:05.466 --> 00:01:10.020
motivation, method, and
then results and discussion.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.910
So let's first motivation.

00:01:14.911 --> 00:01:16.475
As I mentioned,

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:21.010
intra-tumor heterogeneity
is one of the challenging

00:01:21.011 --> 00:01:26.170
things when you want to decipher
tumor evolution for a subject.

00:01:26.171 --> 00:01:28.320
So what happens is
that malignant cells,

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.720
even if they're coming
from a single tumor,

00:01:30.721 --> 00:01:34.240
they'll have a very
distinct genomic profile.

00:01:34.241 --> 00:01:36.810
So in terms of the gene
expression values or copy number

00:01:36.811 --> 00:01:40.760
profile or mutations, each cell
will have something different.

00:01:40.761 --> 00:01:43.424
So earlier,
when people use sequencing,

00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:47.272
they used to do bulk sequencing
where you actually pull a bunch

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:51.046
of cells together and tjem
sequence all the cells together,

00:01:51.047 --> 00:01:53.210
not at a individual level.

00:01:53.211 --> 00:01:57.410
So what happens is that when you
do bulk sequence of such tumors,

00:01:57.411 --> 00:01:59.990
it actually averages out
their genomic profile so

00:01:59.991 --> 00:02:02.610
you lose their
heterogenic information.

00:02:02.611 --> 00:02:05.670
So next came single cell
sequencing, which is

00:02:05.671 --> 00:02:09.330
very important if you want to
unmask what are the underlying

00:02:09.331 --> 00:02:12.640
heterogeneity or difference of
this different malignant cells.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.220
But then single cell data
comes with its own sets of

00:02:17.221 --> 00:02:18.850
computational challenges.

00:02:18.851 --> 00:02:21.310
Since we are sequencing
at single cell, so

00:02:21.311 --> 00:02:24.290
the quantity that you will
sequence is very little, so

00:02:24.291 --> 00:02:27.010
the data is actually
very noisy and sparse.

00:02:27.011 --> 00:02:31.550
By sparse, I mean there will be
a lot of genes that are in low

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:34.360
quantity, so it will not
be detected in many cells.

00:02:34.361 --> 00:02:36.970
So hence, even though it's
expressed in a low level,

00:02:36.971 --> 00:02:40.690
it will be a completely
zero in many cells,

00:02:40.691 --> 00:02:42.640
so there will be drop out and
sparse.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.973
So it becomes very hard to
extract biologically meaningful

00:02:48.974 --> 00:02:49.971
features.

00:02:49.972 --> 00:02:52.540
So that is the expression data.

00:02:52.541 --> 00:02:55.210
Then again comes
the copy number and

00:02:55.211 --> 00:02:58.730
mutation data from
DNA sequencing.

00:02:58.731 --> 00:03:02.429
So to have high fidelity
estimation of mutation and

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.697
copy number profiles at
a single cell level,

00:03:05.698 --> 00:03:08.950
you need very high
depth sequencing.

00:03:08.951 --> 00:03:10.430
So, which is not
always possible.

00:03:10.431 --> 00:03:15.708
Even if it's possible you'll
only be able to sequence only

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:20.680
a couple cells, not a large
cohort to analyze the data.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.370
So here, we're using a
variational autoencoder to learn

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:27.640
a latent representation
of the single cell data

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.590
that is coherent with
the underlying biology, and

00:03:31.591 --> 00:03:35.450
also generalized enough to the
different types of genomic data.

00:03:35.451 --> 00:03:38.660
Different types I mean
Arnesic expression data and

00:03:38.661 --> 00:03:42.810
also copy number data
from DNA sequencing.

00:03:45.544 --> 00:03:49.020
So we know autoencoders are
these multilayered perceptron

00:03:49.021 --> 00:03:52.493
neural networks that learn their
own definition of the data

00:03:52.494 --> 00:03:55.360
in a completely
unsupervised manner.

00:03:55.361 --> 00:03:56.977
But in variational autoencoder,

00:03:56.978 --> 00:03:59.877
the concept of this unsupervised
deep learning is merged with

00:03:59.878 --> 00:04:02.831
that of the Bayesian inference
where you actually, instead of

00:04:02.832 --> 00:04:05.620
generating an unconstrained
representation of the data,

00:04:05.621 --> 00:04:08.130
we assume that the latent
representation is actually

00:04:08.131 --> 00:04:10.110
coming from a underlying
distribution.

00:04:10.111 --> 00:04:14.968
If you think about it, it's like
how scientists observe nature.

00:04:14.969 --> 00:04:17.533
You observe a nature phenomena
and you want to know what is

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:19.618
the underlying law that
is governing them, so

00:04:19.619 --> 00:04:20.747
it's kind of like that.

00:04:20.748 --> 00:04:24.310
Instead of learning
a unconstrained representation,

00:04:24.311 --> 00:04:27.341
we want to learn actually
the distribution that

00:04:27.342 --> 00:04:30.160
the latent representation
is coming from.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.560
So here, this is the structure
of the autoencoder.

00:04:34.561 --> 00:04:37.240
So you have the first input
there is the number of genes.

00:04:37.241 --> 00:04:40.450
And then we have three
intermediate layers, and

00:04:40.451 --> 00:04:42.430
then the last latent layer.

00:04:42.431 --> 00:04:47.150
So how many nodes in each layer
and how many layers you wanted,

00:04:47.151 --> 00:04:49.640
that can be user parameter for
the package.

00:04:49.641 --> 00:04:52.817
So for the results that I will
show, I have used three latent

00:04:52.818 --> 00:04:54.925
dimension in that
particular layers.

00:04:54.926 --> 00:04:58.075
So here, instead of actually
encoding the latent

00:04:58.076 --> 00:05:01.675
representation directly,
we are actually encoding for

00:05:01.676 --> 00:05:05.350
the sample mean and sample
variance, and we're learning

00:05:05.351 --> 00:05:08.127
the The distribution
given the input data.

00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:09.247
Once we have that,

00:05:09.248 --> 00:05:12.420
we can sample Z from that
learned probabilistic.

00:05:12.421 --> 00:05:15.270
So it is kind of giving you
a probabilistic encoding of

00:05:15.271 --> 00:05:17.340
the input data space.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:23.310
And then there is
a complementary decoder network.

00:05:23.311 --> 00:05:26.200
When we learn the structure, we
learn it as a whole encoding and

00:05:26.201 --> 00:05:29.910
decoding, where we are learning
the parameters of the model.

00:05:29.911 --> 00:05:33.501
But we are actually interested
in the latency presentation at

00:05:33.502 --> 00:05:37.239
this, three-dimensional latency
presentation of the data.

00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:44.700
So that is a brief overview of
the algorithm that we are using.

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:48.550
So we have actually had three
types of data sets that we

00:05:48.551 --> 00:05:50.160
have tested our method.

00:05:50.161 --> 00:05:54.381
One, our first gene expression
data says we have three

00:05:54.382 --> 00:05:58.156
different single cell
RNA seq expression data.

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:01.028
The first one is with
over 4,000 cells,

00:06:01.029 --> 00:06:05.942
second one's over 6,000 cells,
and the last one over 2,000, and

00:06:05.943 --> 00:06:07.769
all of them has 23K genes.

00:06:09.723 --> 00:06:11.290
Then we have copy number,

00:06:11.291 --> 00:06:14.450
two copy number datasets
from DNA sequencing data.

00:06:14.451 --> 00:06:18.540
These two are actually
xenograft samples, and

00:06:18.541 --> 00:06:23.770
first sample has 260 and
the second sample is 254 cells.

00:06:23.771 --> 00:06:27.822
These two xenograft samples are
sequenced at a very low depth,

00:06:27.823 --> 00:06:30.050
so 0.05X.

00:06:30.051 --> 00:06:33.200
And we can see that even with
this very low depth copy

00:06:33.201 --> 00:06:34.130
number estimation,

00:06:34.131 --> 00:06:39.520
we can still get some biological
meaningful output from the data.

00:06:39.521 --> 00:06:42.730
And then last,
we wanted to quantify our

00:06:42.731 --> 00:06:46.560
software proposed method with
some other existing techniques.

00:06:46.561 --> 00:06:47.731
So that's why for

00:06:47.732 --> 00:06:51.478
that we have used a synthetic
genetic expression data set

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:55.146
where we know actually how
many clusters are in there and

00:06:55.147 --> 00:06:59.301
can compute some quantitative
metric to analyze the methods.

00:07:01.063 --> 00:07:03.665
So first,
the oligodendroglioma data set,

00:07:03.666 --> 00:07:06.812
where the data set content is
a mixture of malignant and

00:07:06.813 --> 00:07:08.750
non-malignant cells.

00:07:08.751 --> 00:07:11.940
We pass the data through
our autoencoder.

00:07:11.941 --> 00:07:15.360
We can see there's a clear
separation between the malignant

00:07:15.361 --> 00:07:18.587
and the non-malignant
microglia or macrophage cells.

00:07:18.588 --> 00:07:20.309
Next, we wanted to see,

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.430
our goal was to unmask
tumor heterogeneity.

00:07:23.431 --> 00:07:26.490
So we wanted to investigate
more the malignant cells.

00:07:26.491 --> 00:07:29.580
So next, we passed the malignant
cells, the relative expression

00:07:29.581 --> 00:07:31.855
of the malignant cells
only to the autoencoder.

00:07:31.856 --> 00:07:35.609
And we get these nice
structures, so we are getting

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:40.310
a bifurcation-like structure,
a V-like structure.

00:07:40.311 --> 00:07:43.770
Where you see that
cells are put on,

00:07:43.771 --> 00:07:46.910
two different types of
cells are coming out, and

00:07:46.911 --> 00:07:51.280
they're put on the two different
arms of the V structure.

00:07:51.281 --> 00:07:57.260
So the first one, here,
the data that the paper

00:07:57.261 --> 00:08:00.890
originally reported, they also
computed differentiation and

00:08:00.891 --> 00:08:04.050
lineage metric for each of
the cells based on a set of

00:08:04.051 --> 00:08:06.770
signature genes and
a set of control genes.

00:08:06.771 --> 00:08:09.996
So to show that the two
different types of cells

00:08:09.997 --> 00:08:12.808
are indeed put onto
two different arms,

00:08:12.809 --> 00:08:17.205
we have actually colored our
scatter plot using their metric.

00:08:17.206 --> 00:08:20.205
The first one is the lineage
score, which shows which cells

00:08:20.206 --> 00:08:22.810
are oligo-like and
which cells are astro-like.

00:08:22.811 --> 00:08:24.570
These are the two different

00:08:24.571 --> 00:08:26.750
cell types that
are present in the data.

00:08:26.751 --> 00:08:28.320
And we see a nice separation.

00:08:28.321 --> 00:08:32.272
And then we also have the
differentiation metric, that is,

00:08:32.273 --> 00:08:35.137
which cells are further
differentiated and

00:08:35.138 --> 00:08:37.550
which cells are in
more stem-like.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.641
So we do see, so looking at the
structure, we thought the most

00:08:41.642 --> 00:08:44.729
stem-like cells should be near
the neck of the bifurcation.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:49.659
And we do see that the more
stem-like cells are indeed put

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:50.897
in this side.

00:08:50.898 --> 00:08:53.671
So also, this actually
shows how the autoencoder,

00:08:53.672 --> 00:08:56.445
without using the prior
knowledge, we are actually

00:08:56.446 --> 00:08:59.597
fishing out what are the
underlying tumor heterogeneity,

00:08:59.598 --> 00:09:02.640
and how these two populations
are related to each other.

00:09:02.641 --> 00:09:05.700
It's actually showing that even
though there are two different

00:09:05.701 --> 00:09:06.700
cell types present,

00:09:06.701 --> 00:09:08.650
they're actually coming
from the same lineage.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.980
So this is with
the signature genes only.

00:09:11.981 --> 00:09:15.230
Next, we wanted to see
whether we can find

00:09:15.231 --> 00:09:17.710
same kind of structure without
using any signature in it.

00:09:17.711 --> 00:09:19.130
Just automatically selecting

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:25.160
some cells based on maybe
coefficient of variation or

00:09:25.161 --> 00:09:27.030
average expression level or
something.

00:09:27.031 --> 00:09:30.208
So this is based on
average expression level,

00:09:30.209 --> 00:09:34.700
with top 5,000 genes,
5,000 auto-selected genes.

00:09:34.701 --> 00:09:37.648
And we can still see that it
gives a good separation of

00:09:37.649 --> 00:09:40.197
the two different types
of cell population.

00:09:42.187 --> 00:09:45.041
Next, we wanted to compare
with other methods,

00:09:45.042 --> 00:09:48.245
whether they can capture
the same truth or not.

00:09:48.246 --> 00:09:49.644
So the first one is the t-SNE.

00:09:49.645 --> 00:09:54.093
t-SNE is really popular now
to do reduced dimension for

00:09:54.094 --> 00:09:58.728
high dimensional data and has
been used in many single cell

00:09:58.729 --> 00:10:02.435
processing and
RNA seq processing data sets.

00:10:02.436 --> 00:10:05.689
But here, what t-SNE does,
it does show us separation.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:08.605
But it's showing the separation
of different samples that

00:10:08.606 --> 00:10:10.012
the tumors are coming from.

00:10:10.013 --> 00:10:13.739
But it's not actually capturing
what is the state of each cell.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:17.859
It's not showing any distinct
differentiation between the two

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:18.930
types of cells.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.474
Then again, we have the PCA.

00:10:22.475 --> 00:10:25.890
So PCA can show somewhat
separation, but

00:10:25.891 --> 00:10:31.168
the structure is not as distinct
as we have for our autoencoder.

00:10:32.973 --> 00:10:36.314
So next data set, that is,
next data set's the astrocytoma,

00:10:36.315 --> 00:10:38.260
with over 6,000 cells.

00:10:38.261 --> 00:10:41.950
Here again, we see clear
separation of the malignant in

00:10:41.951 --> 00:10:44.870
figure a, malignant and
non-malignant cells.

00:10:44.871 --> 00:10:49.750
And then again, we passed only

00:10:49.751 --> 00:10:53.980
the malignant creative
expressions to the autoencoders.

00:10:53.981 --> 00:10:57.950
And for this particular data
set, the authors didn't compute

00:10:57.951 --> 00:11:00.570
the matrix that we had for
the earlier data set.

00:11:00.571 --> 00:11:03.910
So to know what we
are getting at, I

00:11:03.911 --> 00:11:07.560
plotted it on top of the matrix
from the previous data set.

00:11:07.561 --> 00:11:11.850
So the solid dots actually from
the previous data sets and

00:11:11.851 --> 00:11:14.290
marked with
the differentiation score.

00:11:14.291 --> 00:11:19.706
And we do see that most of
the astrocytomas, the malignant

00:11:19.707 --> 00:11:24.580
astrocytoma cells, are actually
falling in the stem-like region.

00:11:24.581 --> 00:11:28.190
So when I went back to the
original paper to see whether it

00:11:28.191 --> 00:11:28.770
makes sense or

00:11:28.771 --> 00:11:32.840
not, we see that they do mention
that most of the cells in that

00:11:32.841 --> 00:11:35.870
data set is actually in
the more intermediate states.

00:11:35.871 --> 00:11:37.432
So it's actually capturing,

00:11:37.433 --> 00:11:40.203
it's accurately capturing
their statement as well.

00:11:43.259 --> 00:11:47.350
So this is another data set,
which is the leukemia data set.

00:11:47.351 --> 00:11:50.700
And here we see
a separation of cell lines,

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:54.450
K562 cell line and
the other cells.

00:11:54.451 --> 00:11:59.610
But we're not seeing a distinct
separation based on the BCR-ABL.

00:11:59.611 --> 00:12:03.966
So these are gene mutation
status of each cell.

00:12:03.967 --> 00:12:10.076
So we're not seeing a distinct
separation between these status,

00:12:10.077 --> 00:12:13.800
but we do see a blunt
V-like structure.

00:12:13.801 --> 00:12:17.372
Probably it is catching
something else that is common to

00:12:17.373 --> 00:12:18.973
all these type of cells.

00:12:18.974 --> 00:12:22.909
So this particular validation,
it actually needs more

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:27.429
biological analysis to see what
is, we see our trajectory, but

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.540
what are we actually
seeing biologically?

00:12:30.541 --> 00:12:32.990
How relevant it is,
it needs further work.

00:12:32.991 --> 00:12:36.681
I just wanted to show
the plot of this result.

00:12:38.935 --> 00:12:42.821
So next,
we analyzed synthetic data sets.

00:12:42.822 --> 00:12:46.211
So in the synthetic data set,
we have 500 cells,

00:12:46.212 --> 00:12:49.446
3 to 4 each cell,
we have 3,000 genes, and

00:12:49.447 --> 00:12:52.790
cells are coming from
5 different clusters.

00:12:52.791 --> 00:12:55.130
So each cluster will
have 100 cells.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:59.330
So for this particular one,

00:12:59.331 --> 00:13:04.109
there is actually 2.5K cells
that are actually representative

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:07.460
of the cluster and
500 noisy cells.

00:13:07.461 --> 00:13:09.580
When we pass it through
the autoencoder,

00:13:09.581 --> 00:13:13.980
we see a very good separation
of the five clusters.

00:13:13.981 --> 00:13:17.560
And when we estimate
the number of clusters using

00:13:17.561 --> 00:13:18.910
a Gaussian mixture model,

00:13:18.911 --> 00:13:21.912
we can see that the autoencoder
actually correctly predicts

00:13:21.913 --> 00:13:25.431
there are five clusters,
which none of the other ones do.

00:13:25.432 --> 00:13:31.370
t-SNE does capture three,
but the PCA is only one.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:35.950
And also, we computed
the adjusted random index for

00:13:35.951 --> 00:13:37.800
all of the cases.

00:13:37.801 --> 00:13:41.750
And we see that we have
the maximum 0.98 for

00:13:41.751 --> 00:13:45.505
our autoencoder compared
to the other two.

00:13:46.981 --> 00:13:49.574
Next, we increased the number,

00:13:49.575 --> 00:13:53.564
amount of noisy genes
to 2500 out of 3000.

00:13:53.565 --> 00:13:58.076
So only 500 genes are actually
signal of the actual clusters.

00:13:58.077 --> 00:13:59.003
And here,

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:03.848
the autoencoder is actually
picking up four clusters.

00:14:03.849 --> 00:14:08.752
But still, with a much
better adjusted random index

00:14:08.753 --> 00:14:12.545
score than compared
to the rest of ones.

00:14:12.546 --> 00:14:15.495
So you can see PCA
is just a blob.

00:14:15.496 --> 00:14:18.778
t-SNE, some separation
between two, but

00:14:18.779 --> 00:14:21.980
not so much, so
their score is really low.

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:28.696
Then we also did
some comparisons.

00:14:28.697 --> 00:14:34.610
So I showed you what autoencoder
structure we used in our paper.

00:14:34.611 --> 00:14:40.620
And then we tried to see
whether we can, if we

00:14:40.621 --> 00:14:44.050
remove some of the intermediate
layers, how does it affect?

00:14:44.051 --> 00:14:51.018
It does get faster, but
it's not by much, it's 3.43.

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:56.193
And I'd say it's not that
expensive as runtime, but

00:14:56.194 --> 00:15:01.607
we do get a better ARI compared
to the other structures.

00:15:01.608 --> 00:15:05.368
And also, both of those
structures are faster than

00:15:05.369 --> 00:15:08.347
t-SNE, and
t-SNE has slower metric.

00:15:08.348 --> 00:15:11.741
Next star is all that I'm going
to show is the copy number, so

00:15:11.742 --> 00:15:13.539
this is DNA sequencing data, so

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:16.020
the earlier ones were
RNA sequencing data.

00:15:16.021 --> 00:15:20.380
So this is a DNA sequencing
data which I'm showing the two

00:15:20.381 --> 00:15:23.530
samples on top are super
impulse on each other so

00:15:23.531 --> 00:15:27.850
seen on both here xenograft
breast tumor samples.

00:15:27.851 --> 00:15:31.140
You can consider xenograft
four as a later time point of

00:15:31.141 --> 00:15:32.160
xenograft three.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:36.500
What you can see here is
that in xenograft there,

00:15:36.501 --> 00:15:40.320
there are two different clusters
that are one major clusters and

00:15:40.321 --> 00:15:43.010
one minor cluster of the cells.

00:15:43.011 --> 00:15:45.070
And when you progress,
after some treatment,

00:15:45.071 --> 00:15:47.800
when you progress to the later
time point at xenograft four,

00:15:47.801 --> 00:15:51.400
the minor cluster actually
vanishes, so from projecting in

00:15:51.401 --> 00:15:56.600
that space you can clearly
see that which cluster.

00:15:56.601 --> 00:16:00.430
So once we know that, we can
actually study those cells,

00:16:00.431 --> 00:16:03.620
which properties make those
cells not progress into

00:16:03.621 --> 00:16:07.160
the next time points,
but the major clusters.

00:16:07.161 --> 00:16:12.540
And we can see also what is
the levels of this cluster.

00:16:12.541 --> 00:16:14.640
You see this is
the copy number for

00:16:14.641 --> 00:16:17.680
file of xenograft three samples
where you see that there is

00:16:17.681 --> 00:16:23.490
a small cluster of cells that
has like twice the copy number

00:16:23.491 --> 00:16:26.850
in this chromosome compared
to the rest of the cells.

00:16:26.851 --> 00:16:29.350
So that is actually
what is captured

00:16:29.351 --> 00:16:30.820
in the small cluster here.

00:16:30.821 --> 00:16:33.570
So this group of cells
are actually not progressing to

00:16:33.571 --> 00:16:35.680
the next time point.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:41.440
So as I mentioned in the
beginning of the slide one of

00:16:41.441 --> 00:16:45.030
the key problem of single
cell data is drop out so

00:16:45.031 --> 00:16:48.060
it means like some of the genes
will not have any signal in many

00:16:48.061 --> 00:16:48.580
of the cells.

00:16:48.581 --> 00:16:52.600
So we wanted to see how robust
the auto encoder representation

00:16:52.601 --> 00:16:56.500
is compared to
the drop out events.

00:16:56.501 --> 00:17:00.510
So for
that we have used the same

00:17:00.511 --> 00:17:03.510
three data with 2.5 genes
out of 3,000 genes.

00:17:03.511 --> 00:17:09.635
And we can see that both PCA and

00:17:09.636 --> 00:17:13.560
t-SNE after increasing
the number of dropouts

00:17:13.561 --> 00:17:14.800
of couple of percentage.

00:17:14.801 --> 00:17:18.440
They actually doesn't
capture more than one class.

00:17:18.441 --> 00:17:21.336
So, they just capture
just one cluster so

00:17:21.337 --> 00:17:25.474
that's why the ARI score has
dropped down to zero even after

00:17:25.475 --> 00:17:29.658
increasing the percentage of
dropout more than 5% or so.

00:17:29.659 --> 00:17:34.880
But encoder, it is degrading
the performance much,

00:17:34.881 --> 00:17:38.220
but compared to the other two,
it's much better.

00:17:38.221 --> 00:17:42.320
So upon to 10% we still
see close to 0.7, and

00:17:42.321 --> 00:17:46.324
if we increase the number
of dropouts to 50%,

00:17:46.325 --> 00:17:50.048
like half of the genes
are actually dropout.

00:17:50.049 --> 00:17:53.237
And we see, I don't know,
like 0.3 or

00:17:53.238 --> 00:17:57.870
0.4 so it's still
differentiating the clusters.

00:17:57.871 --> 00:18:00.440
Maybe not as well as
the hierarchies but

00:18:00.441 --> 00:18:03.260
its still showing,
it can identify the clusters and

00:18:03.261 --> 00:18:05.790
can somewhat make
some differentiation.

00:18:05.791 --> 00:18:08.690
So compared to the other
two it's much better.

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:13.440
So to summarize
what I have done,

00:18:13.441 --> 00:18:16.750
we have proposed a new way of
extracting useful features with

00:18:16.751 --> 00:18:18.880
biological significance
from single cell data.

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:22.920
Nice thing about is,
it's completely unsupervised and

00:18:22.921 --> 00:18:25.590
it requires minimal
pre-processing of the data.

00:18:25.591 --> 00:18:30.120
It's fairly robust to the
dropouts and we have the package

00:18:30.121 --> 00:18:33.440
and that will be distributed
as an open source package.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:36.300
In future, so

00:18:36.301 --> 00:18:41.460
here all the result I have shown
as three latent dimension.

00:18:41.461 --> 00:18:44.410
I started with three latent
dimension, just to visualize my

00:18:44.411 --> 00:18:47.640
results and see and then I saw
that it's actually showing cited

00:18:47.641 --> 00:18:50.237
and actually go beyond the three
dimension, later point.

00:18:50.238 --> 00:18:53.020
But I would actually
interested to

00:18:53.021 --> 00:18:55.089
investigate latent dimensions.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:59.440
Cuz in the three that I
mentioned we actually can see

00:18:59.441 --> 00:19:01.580
differentiation between
different cell types but

00:19:01.581 --> 00:19:04.710
maybe if we go beyond that maybe
some other dimensions will

00:19:04.711 --> 00:19:07.613
capture some other aspects
of the hydrogeny to suggest

00:19:07.614 --> 00:19:10.870
[INAUDIBLE] environment or
how the cells will respond to

00:19:10.871 --> 00:19:13.920
treatment response and
so on and so forth.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:18.660
And also I showed you
some results that we're

00:19:18.661 --> 00:19:22.170
seeing that kind of trajectory
or differentiation parts but it

00:19:22.171 --> 00:19:26.460
definitely needs more research
on what is the biological

00:19:26.461 --> 00:19:29.660
significance of that structure
that we're learning.

00:19:29.661 --> 00:19:34.040
So, for the CML data, I still
have to do a lot more on that.

00:19:34.041 --> 00:19:37.330
And so these are two immediate

00:19:37.331 --> 00:19:40.480
main goals of the current
work that I want to do.

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:43.330
And so, some references.

00:19:43.331 --> 00:19:47.290
Yeah, that's pretty much
my work that I have done

00:19:47.291 --> 00:19:50.735
in the last 12 weeks,
yeah, okay.

